<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A cargo ship sails due south along a meridian from port <m>P</m> (latitude <m>2^\circ\,10'\,\mathrm{S}</m>) to port <m>Q</m> (latitude <m>4^\circ\,25'\,\mathrm{S}</m>). It leaves at <m>0{6}:0{0}</m> and arrives at <m>{17}:{15}</m>.</p><p>(a) Find the distance it sails, in nautical miles.</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans1"/> nm  </p><p>(b) Find its average speed in knots, correct to 1 decimal place.</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans2"/> knots  </p><p>(c) Find its average speed in km/h, correct to the nearest km/h. Take <m>1</m> knot <m>= 1.853</m> km/h.</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans3"/> km/h  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Both ports are on the same side of the equator, so subtract the latitudes. The difference is <m>{2}^\circ\,{15}' = {135}'</m>, and along a meridian <m>1'</m> is <m>1</m> nm, so the ship sails <m>{135}</m> nm.</p><p>(b) The journey lasts <m>{11}</m> h <m>{15}</m> min <m>= {11} + \tfrac{ {15} }{60} = {11.25}</m> h. A knot is one nautical mile per hour, so the speed is <m>{135} \div {11.25} = {12.000} \approx {12.0}</m> knots.</p><p>(c) <m>{12.000} \times 1.853 = {22.24} \approx {22}</m> km/h.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>135</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>12.0</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>22.236</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>